  "Number": 18,
  "Question": "What is the smallest positive integer \\(n\\) such that \\(\\sqrt n-\\sqrt{n-1}\\lt0.01?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2499\\)",
    "B": "\\(2500\\)",
    "C": "\\(2501\\)",
    "D": "\\(10{,}000\\)",
    "E": "There is no such integer."
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A difference of nearby square roots is easier to compare after multiplying by its conjugate. For a positive integer \\(n\\), both roots are defined and their sum is positive, so\n\\[\n\\sqrt n-\\sqrt{n-1}\n=\\frac{n-(n-1)}{\\sqrt n+\\sqrt{n-1}}\n=\\frac1{\\sqrt n+\\sqrt{n-1}}.\n\\]\nConsequently, the required strict inequality is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\sqrt n+\\sqrt{n-1}>100.\n\\]\nIf \\(n\\le2500\\), then \\(\\sqrt n\\le50\\) and \\(\\sqrt{n-1}<50\\). Their sum is less than 100, so every such positive integer fails.\n\nFor \\(n=2501\\), the sum is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2501}+\\sqrt{2500}=\\sqrt{2501}+50>100.\n\\]\nThus the smallest possible integer is \\(2501\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
